course: dictionary
lesson: 3060
title: Eclipse: one word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Eclipse is a word with more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one. Most of the time it is about the sky. «In a solar eclipse the Moon is between the Sun and the Earth.» Here the Moon moves in front of the Sun and blocks its light.
explain: Read the words around the word to see its meaning. «In a lunar eclipse the Earth is between the Sun and the Moon.» The Moon is the one that goes dark, and the shadow comes from the Earth. Look for words like Sun, Moon and Earth, and you know you are in the sky.
explain: Eclipse can also be a verb, which means to make something seem less important. Imagine a sentence like: «Her goal eclipsed everyone else's that season.» The goal was so much better that the others seemed small. You can test your meaning: put «made less important» in place of eclipsed and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Safety comes first with a solar eclipse. Never look straight at the Sun, even during an eclipse, because it can blind you. Use special eclipse glasses or a pinhole viewer. A lunar eclipse is safe to watch with your eyes.
fact: An eclipse happens when one thing in space passes in front of, or into the shadow of, another.
fact: A «solar eclipse» happens when the Moon passes between the Sun and the Earth and blocks the Sun's light.
fact: A «lunar eclipse» happens when the Earth passes between the Sun and the Moon, and its shadow falls on the Moon.
fact: A lunar eclipse can happen only at full moon, and it is safe to watch. The Moon can look red during it.
fact: Eclipse can also mean to make something seem less important by being much better, as in «eclipsed everyone else».
exercise: «In a solar eclipse the Moon is between the Sun and the Earth.» What is in the middle during a solar eclipse? | *The Moon | The Earth | The Sun | A cloud
exercise: «In a lunar eclipse the Earth is between the Sun and the Moon.» What blocks the Sun's light from reaching the Moon? | *The Earth | A cloud | The Moon | The Sun
exercise: Ruby says, «Look straight at the Sun during the eclipse, it's fine.» What is the safe thing to do during a solar eclipse? | *Use special eclipse glasses or a pinhole viewer | Look straight at the Sun to see the Moon move | Stare at it for a long time without glasses
test: «Her goal in the final eclipsed everyone else's goals that week.» What does eclipsed mean here? | Lost in the crowd | Covered in darkness | Blew up into pieces | *Made the others seem less important by being much better
test: Is it safe to watch a lunar eclipse with your own eyes? | No, never look at it | *Yes, it is safe to watch | Only if you wear special eclipse glasses | Only at noon
exam: The words «total eclipse» and «partial eclipse» tell you what part of the Sun or Moon is blocked: | A total eclipse is safe and a partial eclipse is not | *A total eclipse blocks all of it, and a partial eclipse blocks only part of it | Both words mean the Moon is missing from the sky | Both words mean the same thing, so it does not matter which you read
